fix:
input.js only:

(a) Make a drag that starts on Fire also aim. After `hold('#btn-fire', ...)` (line 91), add:
```js
const fireBtn = el.querySelector('#btn-fire'); let fireAt = null;
fireBtn.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => { fireAt = { id: e.pointerId, x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY }; });
fireBtn.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
  if (!fireAt || e.pointerId !== fireAt.id || !s.active) return;
  s.look.x += (e.clientX - fireAt.x) * 1.6; s.look.y += (e.clientY - fireAt.y) * 1.6;
  fireAt.x = e.clientX; fireAt.y = e.clientY; s.lastLook = now();
});
for (const ev of ['pointerup', 'pointercancel', 'lostpointercapture']) fireBtn.addEventListener(ev, () => { fireAt = null; });
```
The button already has touch-action:none and setPointerCapture, so the moves keep coming even after the thumb slides off it. It isn't inside the canvas, so moves aren't counted twice. Change the touch hint (demos/game.html line 325) and docs/game.md "Phone" to say "hold Fire and slide your thumb to aim while firing".

(b) A still aiming thumb isn't idle. In `s.read()`, before building the return value (around line 106), add `if (aims.size) s.lastLook = now();`. To stop a lost finger staying in `aims` for good (which would then block the easing forever), register `up` on window instead of the canvas: `addEventListener('pointerup', up); addEventListener('pointercancel', up);` in place of line 83. Canvas events bubble up to the window, so nothing else changes.

(c) A sticky lock, where the view eases toward a locked raider while no finger is aiming, is optional extra polish and not needed for this fix. tools/check.mjs should still end with "all good", because its touch steps move and then lift, and __game.step skips read().

In src/game/main.js, right after `const port = makePort(...)` (line 66), add:

  // a phone can drop the drawing context after a long time in the background. three.js puts back everything except
  // the sky light, which is drawn once at start-up, so draw it again.
  canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', () => { if (mode === 'voyage' && !paused) pause(true); });
  canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextrestored', () => {
    const p = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);
    scene.environment = port.scene.environment = p.fromScene(envScene, 0.04, 1, 60000).texture;
    p.dispose();
  });

Notes:
- `pause` and `paused` are defined later in main(), but the listener runs long after main() returns, so the names are already set by then.
- Optionally call pmrem.dispose() after the first fromScene at line 51 too; that generator's helper target is never used again.
- In src/demo/hangar.js, add the same restore listener around lines 123-127, redrawing with fromScene(envScene, 0.04, 1, 4000) into that page's scene.environment.
- Optional guard in tools/check.mjs: on the title screen, call getExtension('WEBGL_lose_context') and run loseContext(), then restoreContext() after about 500 ms. Wait about 1 s, then assert that __game.renderer.properties.get(__game.port.scene.environment).__webglTexture is set and that __game.scene.environment === __game.port.scene.environment.
